WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.540
Secure CMake behavior should not depend on one developer remembering the right command.

2
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:10.680
CI is where approved build profiles become routine and observable.

3
00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:27.100
A strong pipeline can configure with standard presets, build multiple platforms, run tests, use static analysis, exercise sanitizer profiles, verify dependency inputs, inspect artifacts, and preserve logs that explain how the binary was produced.

4
00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:31.460
Verification is not limited to unit tests.

5
00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:36.200
Build logs and compile command databases can confirm which flags were applied.

6
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.140
Package inspections can confirm which files were included.

7
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:43.680
Dependency checks can confirm expected versions and sources.

8
00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:54.420
Exported-symbol checks, license reports, size changes, and reproducibility signals can all help teams notice when a configuration change has broader consequences.

9
00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:58.620
Configuration drift should be treated as an engineering finding.

10
00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:06.320
If a developer profile, CI profile, and release profile produce meaningfully different settings, the difference should be known and justified.

11
00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:15.940
If a cache value or local environment input changes an important result, the team should either control that input or make the build fail with a clear explanation.

12
00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:19.100
Governance means CMake changes receive real review.

13
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:28.720
A build-system edit can change dependencies, hardening, generated code, test coverage, and package contents without touching a C++ source file.

14
00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:37.540
Reviewers should ask what changed in the artifact, what profile was affected, how the change was verified, and whether documentation or release policy needs updating.

15
00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:42.040
Findings should become tracked defects, not informal reminders.
